  Findings (judgement, not violations)                                                                                                                          
  1. Duplicated Code — src/state.ts:215-235 copies 191-213; 276-279 copies 269-273; 239 copies 238. Sole difference is the operator literal, 5 sites. One       
  validateArrayOp(value, schema, path, operator)  deletes ~25 lines with every error string byte-equal, so tests stay green. Repo mirrors  $append              
  deliberately, so call it, not fix it — this is the one spot extraction is clearly better than the mirror.                                                     
  2. Spec over-claim — docs/spec.md:142 now reads "its payload is an array, at every depth, declared or not". Verified:  {meta: {$remove: "x"}}  against a      
  declared object key passes  validatePatch  (null) and merges literally to  meta = {a:1,"$remove":"x"} ; src/state.ts:238-240 rejects only for array-typed     
  keys. Pre-existing for  $append ; this edit extends the claim to  $remove . Narrow the sentence or reject at that path.                                       
  3. Duplicate-value semantics unpinned —  ["a","a","b"]  +  $remove:["a"]  yields  ["b"]  (all occurrences). Correct per "removes matching items", but §6:     
  133 "removes the listed items" reads single-occurrence and no test pins dupes.                                                                                
  4. Style drift (nit) — new suite asserts exact strings via  toBe  (test/state.test.ts:432-456) while the  $append  suite uses  toContain  (368-376).          
  Stricter is better; harmless inconsistency.                                                                                                                   
                                                                                                                                                                
